e,"pf":24.0,"pa":10.0,"off_snaps":68,"epa_per_play":0.1698,"pass_rate":0.5147,"pass_rate_neutral":0.4839,"def_epa_per_play":-0.02593},{"week":14,"opponent":"NYJ","home":true,"pf":20.0,"pa":12.0,"off_snaps":53,"epa_per_play":-0.1042,"pass_rate":0.566,"pass_rate_neutral":0.65,"def_epa_per_play":-0.1687},{"week":15,"opponent":"MIA","home":true,"pf":32.0,"pa":29.0,"off_snaps":71,"epa_per_play":0.1928,"pass_rate":0.5915,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6863,"def_epa_per_play":0.1595},{"week":16,"opponent":"CHI","home":false,"pf":35.0,"pa":13.0,"off_snaps":59,"epa_per_play":0.2171,"pass_rate":0.4746,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5429,"def_epa_per_play":-0.4295},{"week":18,"opponent":"NE","home":true,"pf":35.0,"pa":23.0,"off_snaps":55,"epa_per_play":0.09968,"pass_rate":0.6,"pass_rate_neutral":0.625,"def_epa_per_play":-0.03686}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2024,"team":"DAL","distance":0.7374,"shared":21,"play_caller":"Mike McCarthy"},{"season":2024,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.7534,"shared":21,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"HOU","distance":0.7674,"shared":21,"play_caller":"Nick Caley"},{"season":2025,"team":"JAX","distance":0.7789,"shared":21,"play_caller":"Liam Coen"},{"season":2025,"team":"DAL","distance":0.7905,"shared":21,"play_caller":"Brian Schottenheimer"}],"defense":[{"season":2021,"team":"IND","distance":0.7799,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"NO","distance":0.8274,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dennis Allen"},{"season":2024,"team":"CHI","distance":0.8525,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"PHI","distance":0.8778,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.8798,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Jeff Ulbrich"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Bills went 13-3 in 2022 and outscored opponents 455 to 286. Both sides of the ball were strong, with the offense 2nd in EPA per play and the defense 7th in EPA allowed. Their most distinctive traits were the 2nd lowest 12 personnel usage and the highest nickel usage in the league.","offense":"The Bills had one of the most efficient offenses in the league, ranking in the top eight in four of the five main efficiency measures. They threw more than almost anyone relative to the situation, even when the score did not call for it. Schematically, they stood out for the 2nd lowest 12 personnel usage and a number of tight ends on the field that was among the lowest in the league. Josh Allen ran the passing game and leaned heavily on Stefon Diggs, who drew 28% of the targets.","defense":"The Bills defense was well above average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was EPA allowed per rush, where it ranked 5th, and its weakest was explosive play rate allowed, where it ranked 23rd. Gregory Rousseau led the team with 8 sacks, and Von Miller added 8, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Schematically, they had the highest nickel usage in the league.","special":"The Bills were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 87% of field goals, which was a little above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the quarterback, about 17% of the money on the books. The offense also brought back more of its roster than most, with 84% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Josh Allen played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 4,283 yards. Stefon Diggs was the clear top target with 28% of the looks and 1,429 receiving yards. Devin Singletary handled most of the work in the backfield with 59% of the carries. On defense, Gregory Rousseau led the team with 8 sacks and Jordan Poyer picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2024 Cowboys under Mike McCarthy and the 2024 Jets. Their defense looked most like the 2021 Colts and the 2019 Saints under Dennis Allen.","trends":"The offense went from 4th in EPA per play in 2021 to 2nd in 2022, about where it was. The defense moved from 1st in EPA allowed per play in 2021 to 7th in 2022, a modest decline. The biggest change in approach was empty formation usage, which went from 7% to 11%.","notes":"2022 is the first season with every data source available, including FTN's play design charting. It is also the last season that used the older formation labels, so singleback and I formation rates still appear here."}}